From 4e1152ac4eb39285fc726011f75a083b1c797acf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Geonmo Ryu <geonmo@kisti.re.kr>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 12:00:13 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] nfs, xrootd: report the capacity of the pools or a space
 reservation

Motivation:
Clients can't see how much space dCache has. The NFS door reports a
fixed size of 1 EiB, so df on an NFS mount is of no use to users or
monitoring. The xrootd door doesn't answer kXR_Qspace at all, so
`xrdfs spaceinfo` fails and df hides xrootdfs mounts (size 0).

Modification:
Add dcache.capacity.source, inherited by nfs.capacity.source and
xrootd.capacity.source:

- namespace (default): a fixed 1 EiB, as the NFS door reports today
- pools: the total and free space of the pools that files in the
  queried path are first written to, counting removable replicas as
  free, as the frontend does for SRR; 1 EiB if no write link matches.
  With space reservation, link groups are chosen as the spacemanager
  does: that of the WriteToken, or else a matching one as if unreserved
  uploads to link groups were allowed, since doors can't see that
  spacemanager setting
- reservation: the size and available space of the space reservation
  given by the WriteToken tag of the queried path, as the FTP door does
  for SITE USAGE, or as 'pools' for paths without one

Capacity.Provider computes the value for both doors and logs a failure
once, until the capacity can be determined again. The xrootd door
answers kXR_Qspace in the format of xrootd's oss plugin for paths the
user can access. As nfs4j doesn't pass the queried file to the door,
the NFS door reports all pools for 'pools' and needs a space token in
nfs.capacity.reservation for 'reservation', or it fails to start. It
keeps the namespace file counts and falls back to the namespace
statistics on failure.

Result:
By default, NFS is unchanged, and xrootd clients now see 1 EiB, so df
shows xrootdfs mounts. Disk-only sites can report the capacity of their
pools, and sites using space reservations the capacity of a reservation.

